# Atomic thread flags (OpenSpec change wasm-pointer-width, design D7). libqb's threads (QBMAIN, MAIN_LOOP, the timer
# thread) hand work to each other through plain flags that one sets and another spins on or polls (study 03,
# section 1.5). That holds on x86, which orders memory strongly; WebAssembly's threads and ARM do not. Each flag
# becomes a std::atomic of its type, with its name: every read and store in libqb and in the generated C++
# compiles as before. On every platform, so that one form of libqb is tested.
# This file: suspend_program and stop_program. qbevent stays a plain uint32: the generated C++ tests it between
# all statements, and as a std::atomic a loop of 400 million statements took 4.6 s where it took 1.4 s (measured
# 2026-11-20, the program's C++ is built without optimization, so each test was a call).
file: internal/c/qbx.cpp
--- old
uint32 qbevent = 0;
uint8 suspend_program = 0;
uint8 stop_program = 0;
--- new
uint32 qbevent = 0;
std::atomic<uint8> suspend_program = 0;
std::atomic<uint8> stop_program = 0;
--- end
